Modernity in China is a complex interplay of adopting Western ideas and adapting them to fit within the context of Chinese culture and history. This process has been ongoing since the late 19th century when China first encountered the West's industrial and technological advancements. The concept of modernity in China is not just about adopting new technologies or political systems but also about redefining what it means to be Chinese in a rapidly changing world.

